    note: `The integration is software that touches money and physical goods, so it is priced from ${P.software} and gets a written specification before a figure. A full store with the shipping layer designed in from the start begins at ${P.shop}. A plain catalogue or brochure site with no dispatch logic sits in the static range from ${P.site}, and ongoing search work starts from ${P.seo}. Within a shipping project, the lines that move the price are predictable: cash on delivery reconciliation is almost always the largest, returns and the reverse leg the second largest, and cross-border paperwork the third. Rates and waybills, which is what people ask for, are usually the cheapest part. What none of our figures include is anything Aramex charges: tariffs, fuel and remote-area surcharges, COD handling arrangements, insurance, minimum volumes and account terms are commercial matters between you and the courier. They differ by account and they change, so we will not restate them even as an estimate. Get them in writing, put them in the rate model, and decide with real numbers. Our quote is itemised in USD, staged against milestones, and nothing is billed before you approve it.`,
